<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oslofjord</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oslofjord is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, stretching about 100 kilometres north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. Its western shore held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> centres of Vestfold, with the ship burials of Oseberg and Gokstad, and its eastern side the district of Vingulmark. At its narrows by Drøbak, Norwegian guns sank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-cruiser-blucher">German cruiser Blücher</a> in April 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oslofjord.jpg" alt="Oslofjord" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: © 2005 by Tomasz Sienicki [user: tsca, mail: tomasz.sienicki at gmail.com] · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OSLO-NO-04_05_ubt.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
